            { "What is the Sun made of?", "The Sun is made mostly of two gases, hydrogen and helium. It is so hot inside that the gas turns into plasma, a glowing gas that carries electricity. The Sun’s own gravity pulls all this gas inward and holds it together in a ball. Gravity is the pull that draws things toward each other, the same pull that makes a dropped spoon fall to the floor.",
                "The Sun is huge compared with Earth. It is about 100 times wider, so if Earth were a pea, the Sun would be about as wide as an open umbrella. About 1.3 million Earths could fit inside it. The Sun also holds almost all the stuff in our solar system, 99.8 percent of it. All the planets, moons, and space rocks together make up the tiny bit that is left.",
                wow = "The Sun is a ball of glowing gas, held together by its own gravity." },
            { "Where does the Sun’s energy come from?", "The Sun’s energy comes from its core, the hot, heavy center. The core is about 15 million degrees Celsius, far hotter than the surface. There, the Sun’s weight squeezes the gas so hard that tiny pieces of hydrogen smash together and join to make helium. This joining is called nuclear fusion, and it gives off huge amounts of energy.",
                "The energy pushes outward, and that push keeps the Sun from collapsing under its own weight. Getting out of the crowded inside is slow, because the energy keeps bumping into gas on its way. It takes about 170,000 years to travel from the core to near the surface. That is far longer than the oldest pyramids have stood. Once it escapes as sunlight, though, it races to Earth in about eight minutes.",
                wow = "The Sun’s core is about 15 million degrees Celsius." },
            { "Why do we have day and night?", "Day and night happen because Earth spins like a top. It turns all the way around about once every 24 hours. When your side of Earth faces the Sun, it is day. As your side turns away, the Sun seems to sink below the edge of the sky, and night begins.",
                "The Sun does not really move across the sky. It only looks that way, just as trees seem to rush past when you ride in a car. At the same time, Earth travels around the Sun in a big loop called an orbit. The Sun’s gravity holds Earth on this path, and one trip around takes one year. So every birthday you celebrate means Earth has circled the Sun once more.",
                wow = "Earth spins all the way around about once every 24 hours.",
                try = "Shine a flashlight on a ball in a dark room. Turn the ball slowly. One side has day, and the other has night." },
            { "What is the glowing loop in the picture?", "The glowing loop in the picture is called a prominence. A prominence is a giant cloud of hot, glowing gas that floats above the Sun’s surface. It is held up by the Sun’s magnetism, the same invisible force that makes a magnet stick to a fridge. Because the Sun’s magnetism twists into loops, the gas often curves into an arch.",
                "The gas in a prominence is a little cooler and thicker than the gas around it. Sometimes a prominence breaks loose and flings a cloud of gas out into space. If that cloud reaches Earth, it can make the northern and southern lights glow in the night sky. Scientists watch the Sun with special telescopes, so they can see these storms coming. Never try this yourself, because looking at the Sun can hurt your eyes, even with sunglasses.", image = art("sun", "A loop of glowing gas rises above the Sun’s surface.", sunSpots),
                wow = "The Sun’s magnetism can hold a giant cloud of gas above its surface." },
        },
        sources = {
            "https://science.nasa.gov/sun/",
            "https://science.nasa.gov/sun/facts/",
            "https://howthingsfly.si.edu/ask-an-explainer/how-long-does-it-take-light-sun-get-earth",
        },
    },
    {
        id = "mars",
        related = "moon",
        category = "Space",
        title = "Mars",
        question = "Why is Mars red?",
        keywords = "planet red rover robot solar system dust rust Perseverance",
        image = art("mars", "A rover rolls across the rusty ground on Mars.", {
            { 0.53, 0.17, "Cameras", "The rover’s eyes for exploring the ground." },
            { 0.47, 0.68, "Wheels", "A rover is a robot car, built to drive over rocky ground." },
            { 0.88, 0.62, "Rusty rock", "Rocks and dust full of rust make Mars red." },
            { 0.18, 0.12, "Dusty sky", "Strong winds lift fine red dust high into the air." },
        }),
        sections = {
            { "Why is Mars red?", "Mars is red because its rocks and dust are full of rust. The ground holds a lot of iron. When iron reacts with oxygen, it turns into rust, which is reddish brown. A bike left out in the rain rusts in the same way. Rust is a kind of iron oxide.",
                "Mars is also very dusty, and strong winds lift the fine dust high into the air. Because the dust floats in the sky, the whole planet glows red when we see it from far away. From Earth, you can sometimes spot Mars at night as a reddish dot. So when you see red Mars, you are really looking at rust.",
                wow = "Mars is covered in rust, the same stuff that forms on an old bike." },
            { "What is the weather like on Mars?", "Mars is much colder than Earth because it is farther from the Sun. On average, it is about minus 85 degrees Fahrenheit there. That is much colder than the inside of your freezer at home. The air is also very thin and made mostly of carbon dioxide, the gas you breathe out. Thin air cannot hold heat, so warmth escapes quickly at night.",
                "Even thin air can blow hard, though. Strong winds sometimes whip up dust storms so big that they cover the whole planet. The dust can take months to settle again. Smaller spinning dust storms, called dust devils, look like little tornadoes. A visitor would need a space suit for air and warmth.",
                wow = "On average, Mars is about minus 85 degrees Fahrenheit. That is much colder than a freezer." },
            { "How long is a day on Mars?", "A day on Mars is a little longer than a day on Earth. Mars spins around once in about 24 hours and 40 minutes. A Mars day has its own name, a sol. If you lived there, you would get about 40 extra minutes each day.",
                "A year on Mars is much longer, though. Mars takes 687 Earth days to go once around the Sun, because it travels a bigger circle. That is almost two of our years, so you would have a birthday only every other year! Mars also has two small moons, called Phobos and Deimos. They are shaped like lumpy potatoes, not round like our Moon.",
                wow = "A day on Mars is called a sol. It is about 40 minutes longer than ours." },
            { "Was there ever water on Mars?", "Yes, long ago Mars had water flowing on its ground. Today, we can still see the marks it left behind. There are dry river valleys and old lakebeds. Scientists have also found minerals that only form in water. Some marks show huge floods from about 3.5 billion years ago.",
                "Mars is too cold for lakes now, so most of its water is frozen. Ice lies just under the ground near the north and south poles. A little water also floats in thin clouds. Scientists study this old water because living things on Earth need water. They want to know whether tiny living things ever lived on Mars too.",
                wow = "Dry river valleys and old lakebeds still mark the ground on Mars." },
            { "What do robots do on Mars?", "Robots on Mars explore the ground and study its rocks. The robots that drive around are called rovers. A rover is a robot car with wheels, cameras, and science tools. The rover Perseverance is about the size of a car. It landed on Mars on February 18, 2021, after a trip of about seven months.",
                "It landed in Jezero Crater, a giant bowl about 28 miles wide. Billions of years ago, a river ran into this bowl and filled it with a lake. Perseverance drills into the rocks there and stores small samples in tubes. Scientists hope a future mission can bring them to Earth. A small helicopter named Ingenuity rode along and made the first powered flight on another planet.",
                wow = "The rover Perseverance is about the size of a car. Its trip to Mars took about seven months." },
        },
        sources = {
            "https://science.nasa.gov/mars/",
            "https://science.nasa.gov/mars/facts/",
