            "https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/news/touchdown-nasas-mars-perseverance-rover-safely-lands-on-red-planet/",
        },
    },
    {
        id = "volcanoes",
        related = "earth-layers",
        category = "Earth",
        title = "Volcanoes",
        question = "What is inside a volcano?",
        keywords = "lava magma eruption mountain rock ash fire island Hawaii volcanologist",
        cover = volcano.path,
        image = art("volcano-cutaway", "Magma rises inside the volcano. Once it flows out of the vent, we call it lava."),
        sections = {
            { "Where does magma come from?", "Magma comes from deep inside Earth, many tens of miles under the ground. Down there, it is hot enough to melt some of the rock. Magma is a mix of melted rock, tiny crystals, and gas. Because magma is lighter than the solid rock around it, it slowly rises, a bit like a bubble rising in water.",
                "On its way up, magma can pool in a space under a volcano called a magma chamber. Gas in the magma pushes outward, like the fizz in a shaken bottle of soda. When the push grows strong enough, magma finds a weak spot and breaks through. The opening where it comes out is called a vent. That is the moment a volcano erupts.",
                wow = "Magma rises because it is lighter than the solid rock around it." },
            { "Why are some eruptions gentle and others explosive?", "Some eruptions are gentle because their lava is runny and gas can escape easily. Then lava pours out and flows downhill in glowing rivers. Mauna Loa in Hawaiʻi erupts like this. People can usually walk away from these slow lava flows.",
                "Other eruptions are explosive, because gas is trapped and blasts the magma into pieces. These pieces can shoot up to 20 miles into the sky, more than twice as high as jet planes fly. The smallest pieces are volcanic ash, made of tiny bits of rock and glass. When Mount St. Helens in Washington exploded in 1980, its ash landed as far as 370 miles away. Wind can even carry the finest ash around the world.", image = volcano,
                wow = "People can usually walk away from slow lava flows." },
            { "How hot is lava?", "The hottest lava is about 2,140 degrees Fahrenheit. A kitchen oven at full heat reaches only about one quarter of that. Lava like this can melt gold, silver, and even iron. That is why fresh lava glows orange and red, just like the glowing wires in a toaster.",
                "As lava flows, its outside cools and turns dark first. Inside, it may stay hot and runny for a long time. When it cools completely, it becomes new rock. Sometimes it cools so fast that it turns into obsidian, a shiny black glass. Obsidian edges can be sharper than steel. So hard, dark rock near a volcano may be lava from long ago.",
                wow = "Hot lava can melt gold, silver, and even iron." },
            { "Can a volcano build an island?", "Yes, every one of the main Hawaiian Islands was built by volcanoes. They grew over a hot spot, a very hot place deep under the sea floor. Each eruption added a new layer of lava rock. After many eruptions, the volcano grew tall enough to rise out of the ocean.",
                "Mauna Loa on the Island of Hawaiʻi is the largest active volcano in the world. Its neighbor, Kīlauea, is one of the busiest volcanoes on Earth. Over time, volcanic rock breaks down into very rich soil. That is why plants and farms can grow so well near old volcanoes.",
                wow = "Every one of the main Hawaiian Islands was built by volcanoes." },
            { "How do scientists know a volcano may erupt?", "Scientists know because a volcano often gives warning signs before it erupts. These scientists are called volcanologists. As magma moves up, it cracks rock and causes many small earthquakes. Machines called seismometers feel this shaking, even when people cannot.",
                "Rising magma can also make the ground swell up, like a balloon slowly filling. GPS sensors measure these tiny changes in the ground. Other tools sniff the gases coming out of the volcano. In the United States alone, scientists keep watch on more than 160 volcanoes this way. Their warnings help people get away safely in time.",
                wow = "Machines called seismometers feel shaking that people cannot." },
        },
        sources = {
            "https://www.usgs.gov/programs/VHP/about-volcanoes",
            "https://volcanoes.usgs.gov/volcanic_ash/ash.html",
            "https://www.usgs.gov/observatories/calvo/news/how-hot-hot-when-it-comes-volcanoes",
            "https://www.usgs.gov/programs/VHP/comprehensive-monitoring-provides-timely-warnings-volcano-reawakening",
            "https://kids.nationalgeographic.com/science/article/volcano",
        },
    },
    {
        id = "rainbows",
        related = "water",
        category = "Science",
        title = "Rainbows",
        question = "Where do rainbows come from?",
        keywords = "light color rain sun prism weather sky spectrum double rainbow refraction",
        image = art("rainbows", "Look closely: red is on the outside of the rainbow, and violet is on the inside.", {
            { 0.30, 0.24, "Red", "Red light is always on the outside of the main bow." },
            { 0.72, 0.44, "Violet", "Violet light is always on the inside." },
            { 0.50, 0.52, "Rain", "Millions of raindrops shine together to paint the bow." },
        }),
        sections = {
            { "What is sunlight made of?", "Sunlight is made of many colors mixed together. When they are all mixed, our eyes see plain white light. When something spreads them apart, we see a band of colors called a spectrum. People often list seven colors: red, orange, yellow, green, blue, indigo, and violet.",
                "In real life, the colors have no sharp edges. Each one slowly melts into the next, so there are many more shades than seven. Many people today leave out indigo and just say blue and violet. A glass prism can spread sunlight into a spectrum, and so can a single raindrop. That is where every rainbow begins.",
                wow = "White sunlight is really many colors mixed together." },
            { "How does a raindrop make a rainbow?", "A raindrop makes a rainbow by bending and bouncing sunlight. First, light bends as it goes from the air into the water. This bending is called refraction. Each color bends a slightly different amount, so the colors begin to fan out. Then the light hits the back of the drop and reflects, which means it bounces back.",
                "As the light leaves the drop, it bends once more, and the colors spread even wider. Red light comes out at a slightly bigger angle than violet light. That is why red is always on the outside of the bow and violet is on the inside. One raindrop is smaller than a pea, but millions of them shine together. Together they paint one huge arc across the sky.",
                wow = "Each color of light bends a slightly different amount inside a raindrop." },
            { "When and where can you see a rainbow?", "To see a rainbow, the Sun must be behind you and raindrops must be in front of you. The bright light comes back toward you at an angle of about 42 degrees. That is a little less than halfway between flat ground and straight up. Because of this angle, the Sun must be low in the sky.",
                "If the Sun is more than 42 degrees above the ground, no rainbow can be seen at all. That is why rainbows are easiest to spot in the early morning or late afternoon. At sunset, you can see a full half circle. From a tall building or an airplane, you may even see the whole circle, because the ground no longer hides the bottom part.",
                wow = "To see a rainbow, the Sun must be behind you." },
            { "Why do some rainbows come in pairs?", "Some rainbows come in pairs because light can bounce twice inside a raindrop. The first bounce makes the main, bright rainbow. A second bounce makes a second rainbow higher up in the sky. Each bounce lets some light escape, so the second rainbow is always fainter.",
                "The second rainbow has a surprise, too. Its colors are in the opposite order, with red on the inside and violet on the outside. Between the two bows, the sky looks darker than the sky around it. This dark strip is called Alexander’s band. If you see one rainbow, always look a little higher for its twin.",
                wow = "A second rainbow forms when light bounces twice inside a raindrop." },
            { "Can you make a rainbow?", "Yes, you can make your own rainbow on a sunny day. Stand with your back to the Sun, so your shadow points away from it. Then spray a fine mist of water from a garden hose in front of you. The tiny drops in the mist act just like raindrops.",
                "Watch the mist carefully, and a small rainbow may appear. You can also make colors indoors. Put a glass of water on a sunny windowsill, and look for a band of colors on the floor or wall. Each one is a tiny spectrum, made the same way as a real rainbow.",
                try = "On a sunny day, stand with your back to the Sun. Spray a fine mist from a garden hose and watch for colors." },
        },
        sources = {
            "https://www.metoffice.gov.uk/weather/learn-about/weather/optical-effects/rainbows",
            "https://www.weather.gov/fgz/Rainbow",
        },
    },
    {
        id = "magnets",
        related = "earth-layers",
        category = "Science",
        title = "Magnets",
        question = "How does a magnet pull?",
        keywords = "magnet metal iron compass north south pull push force magnetic field poles lodestone electromagnet",
        image = art("magnets", "Steel paper clips stick to the ends of a horseshoe magnet.", {
            { 0.23, 0.47, "Pole", "The pull is strongest at a magnet’s ends." },
            { 0.60, 0.68, "Steel paper clip", "Steel contains a lot of iron, so the magnet holds it." },
            { 0.85, 0.75, "Too far away", "The pull gets weaker farther from the magnet." },
        }),
        sections = {
            { "How can a magnet pull without touching?", "A magnet can pull without touching because a magnetic field spreads out around it. A magnetic field is the space around a magnet where its force works. A force is a push or a pull, and you cannot see this one. You can still notice it, though, when a paper clip slides toward a magnet across the table.",
                "The field is strongest close to the magnet’s ends, called poles, and gets weaker farther away. That is why a paper clip jumps only when it gets close. The force can even work through thin things. For example, a magnet under a sheet of paper can drag a paper clip on top of it. In the same way, a fridge magnet holds a drawing tight against the door.",
                wow = "A magnet’s force reaches out into the space around it. You cannot see it." },
            { "Why do some metals stick to magnets?", "Some metals stick because of how their tiniest parts are lined up. Iron, nickel, and cobalt are strongly magnetic. Inside them, tiny particles called electrons spin like little tops. In these metals, most of the electrons spin the same way, so their pull adds up. Steel contains a lot of iron, so magnets stick to steel too.",
                "In most other things, the tiny spins point every which way and cancel each other out. That is why wood, plastic, aluminum cans, and copper coins do not stick. Iron can even become a magnet itself. If you rub an iron nail along a magnet many times, its tiny parts start to line up. Then the nail can pick up a paper clip on its own.",
                wow = "Inside iron, tiny particles called electrons spin like little tops.",
                try = "Test things around your home with a fridge magnet. Sort them into two piles: sticks, and does not stick." },
            { "Why do magnets push and pull each other?", "Magnets push and pull because every magnet has two poles, north and south. Opposite poles attract, which means they pull toward each other. Matching poles repel, which means they push each other away. So a north pole and a south pole snap together, but two north poles push apart.",
                "You can feel this for yourself with two bar magnets. Turn one so that two north ends face each other, then slowly push them together. They seem to slide away, as if an invisible cushion sits between them. Now turn one magnet around, and they click together at once. Engineers use this push and pull in many machines, from toys to speakers.",
                wow = "Two north poles push apart, but a north pole and a south pole snap together." },
            { "Why does a compass point north?", "A compass points north because Earth itself is a giant magnet. Deep inside Earth is a hot, liquid layer called the outer core. Scientists think moving melted metal there makes Earth’s magnetic field. Invisible lines of this field run between Earth’s two magnetic poles, near the North and South Poles.",
                "A compass needle is a small magnet that can spin, so it lines up with these lines. Long ago, people in China found that a needle rubbed on a lodestone, a natural magnet rock, would point north and south. Some animals can sense Earth’s magnetic field too. Pigeons, bees, and salmon may use it to find their way. With a compass, you can find north just like they do.",
                wow = "Earth itself is a giant magnet." },
            { "Can electricity make a magnet?", "Yes, electricity can make a magnet. When electricity flows through a coil of wire, the coil becomes a magnet. This kind of magnet is called an electromagnet. When the electricity is switched off, its magnetism disappears.",
                "That switch makes electromagnets very useful. For example, a big electromagnet on a crane can lift heavy iron and steel scraps, then let go when the power goes off. Electromagnets also work inside speakers, electric motors, and doorbells. Many machines in your home have one hidden inside. So magnets are not just toys on the fridge. They help run the world around you every day.",
                wow = "An electromagnet can be switched on and off." },
        },
        sources = {
            "https://www.britannica.com/science/magnet",
            "https://education.nationalgeographic.org/resource/magnetism/",
            "https://science.howstuffworks.com/electromagnet.htm",
        },
    },
    {
        id = "seeds",
        related = "bees",
        category = "Plants",
        title = "Seeds",
        question = "What is inside a seed?",
        keywords = "plant grow sprout garden flower tree root soil germination dispersal fruit",
        cover = sprout.path,
        image = art("seed-cutaway", "Inside a bean seed, the coat protects the baby plant and its stored food."),
        sections = {
            { "What are the parts of a seed?", "Every seed has three main parts: a baby plant, a food store, and a coat. The baby plant already has a tiny root and a tiny shoot, folded up tight. Its food is often packed into two thick seed leaves. In a bean, the two halves you can pull apart are those seed leaves, full of food.",
                "The seed coat works like a raincoat and a suit of armor at once. It keeps the baby plant from drying out, and it guards against bugs and bumps. Because the coat seals the seed so well, the seed can rest for months or longer. That is why farmers can keep dry seeds in sacks all winter and plant them in spring.",
                wow = "A seed already holds a baby plant, with a tiny root and a tiny shoot." },
            { "How does a seed wake up?", "A seed wakes up when it gets water, air, and the right warmth. First it soaks up water and swells, until its coat splits open. This first growth is called germination. The tiny root comes out first and grows down, because the young plant needs water and something to hold it in place.",
                "Next the shoot pushes up through the soil toward the light. In many plants, such as beans, the shoot comes up bent like a hook, which protects its tender tip as it pushes through the dirt. Once the first leaves open, the plant can make its own food from sunlight. From then on it no longer needs the food packed inside the seed.", image = sprout,
                wow = "The tiny root comes out first and grows down.",
                try = "Put a dry bean on a wet paper towel in a jar. Keep the towel damp, and watch for the root." },
            { "How do seeds travel?", "Seeds travel by wind, water, and animals, because plants cannot walk. Moving seeds away from the parent plant is called seed dispersal. It matters because a seed that lands right under its parent would have to share light, water, and soil. Far away, it has a better chance of finding room to grow.",
                "Each plant has its own trick. Dandelion and milkweed seeds carry fluffy parachutes that ride the wind. Burrs have tiny hooks that cling to fur, and they gave an inventor the idea for Velcro. Coconuts float, so the sea can carry them to a far beach. Squirrels bury acorns and forget some of them, and those acorns can grow into new oak trees.",
                wow = "A seed that lands under its parent has to share light, water, and soil." },
            { "Why do some plants grow fruit around their seeds?", "Many plants wrap their seeds in sweet fruit, so that animals will carry them. A bird eats a berry and flies away. Later the seeds come out in its droppings, often far from the parent plant. The hard seed coat keeps the seed safe on its trip through the bird.",
                "You eat seed packages too. An apple has small brown seeds in its core, and a tomato is full of tiny seeds. A peach hides its seed inside a hard pit. Next time you eat fruit, look for the seeds and count them. Each one holds a baby plant that could grow into a whole new plant.",
                wow = "A seed can ride inside a bird and come out far from its parent plant." },
        },
        sources = {
            "https://www.britannica.com/science/seed-plant-reproductive-part",
            "https://www.britannica.com/science/germination",
            "https://kids.britannica.com/students/article/seed/276972",
            "https://bio.libretexts.org/Courses/Lumen_Learning/Biology_for_Majors_II_(Lumen)/12%3A_Module_9-_Plant_Reproduction/12.17%3A_Fruit_and_Seed_Dispersal",
            "https://www.nationalgeographic.com/science/article/2000-year-old-phoenix-seed-rises-from-the-ashes",
        },
    },
    {
        id = "heart",
        category = "Your Body",
        title = "Your Heart",
        question = "What does your heart do all day?",
        keywords = "body blood muscle beat pulse health exercise chambers valves lungs oxygen",
        image = art("heart", "Put a hand on your chest. Can you feel your heart beating?", {
            { 0.52, 0.67, "Heart", "Under this hand, four chambers pump blood." },
            { 0.50, 0.09, "Brain", "The left side of the heart pumps blood all the way up here." },
            { 0.30, 0.85, "Arteries and veins", "Tubes that carry blood away from the heart and back again." },
        }),
        sections = {
            { "What is inside your heart?", "Your heart has four rooms inside, called chambers. The two top chambers collect blood as it flows in from your body and lungs. The two bottom chambers are the strong pumps that push blood back out. A wall of muscle runs down the middle and keeps the right side and the left side apart.",
                "Between the chambers sit valves, which work like doors that swing only one way. They open to let blood pass, then snap shut so it cannot flow backward. When they close, they make the lub-dub sound. The lub is the first set of doors shutting, and the dub is the second. Doctors listen to these sounds to check how well your heart is working.",
                wow = "Your heart has four rooms, with a wall of muscle down the middle." },
            { "Why does blood go to your lungs?", "Blood goes to your lungs to pick up oxygen. Oxygen is a gas in the air that every part of your body needs to stay alive. The right side of your heart pumps tired blood, which has used up its oxygen, to your lungs. There the blood picks up fresh oxygen from the air you breathe in.",
                "Then the blood flows back into the left side of the heart. The left side pumps it out to your brain, your stomach, and your toes. Tubes called arteries carry blood away from the heart, and veins bring it back. So each drop of blood makes a figure-eight loop, first to the lungs and then around the body.",
                wow = "The right side of your heart sends blood to your lungs to pick up oxygen." },
            { "What makes your heart beat?", "Your heart makes its own tiny electric signal, and that signal starts every beat. It begins in a small patch of special cells in the top right chamber. This patch is called the heart’s natural pacemaker. The signal spreads across the top chambers, so they squeeze first and push blood down into the bottom chambers.",
                "Then the signal pauses for a moment, so the bottom chambers can fill up. Next it races down their walls, and they squeeze blood out of the heart. The whole beat is over in less than a second, before you could say “heartbeat.” Then the heart relaxes, the signal starts again, and the next beat begins.",
                wow = "Your heart makes its own tiny electric signal for every beat." },
            { "Why does your heart beat faster when you run?", "Your heart beats faster when you run, because your muscles need more oxygen. Working muscles use up oxygen quickly, so your heart must deliver blood more often. For a grown-up at rest, a normal heart rate is 60 to 100 beats in one minute. That means about one or two beats each time a clock ticks one second.",
                "You can measure your own heart rate, called your pulse. Press two fingers gently on the inside of your wrist and count the beats for 30 seconds. Double that number to get your beats in one minute. Then run in place for a minute and count again. Your number will jump, and it slowly drops back as you rest. Playing and exercise help keep your heart strong.",
                wow = "A grown-up’s resting heart beats 60 to 100 times in one minute." },
        },
        sources = {
            "https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/heart/anatomy",
            "https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/heart/blood-flow",
            "https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/heart/heart-beats",
        },
    },
    {
        id = "pyramids",
        related = "dinosaurs",
        category = "Long Ago",
        title = "The Pyramids",
        question = "How did Egyptians build the pyramids?",
        keywords = "egypt egyptian giza khufu pharaoh limestone ramp sledge nile ancient builders tomb quarry merer",
        image = art("pyramids", "An artist’s idea of how a crew might have pulled a stone block up a ramp at Giza.", {
            { 0.18, 0.56, "Stone block", "Many blocks weighed nearly as much as two cars." },
            { 0.07, 0.80, "Sledge", "A wooden sled with runners that slide over the ground." },
            { 0.40, 0.60, "Ropes", "Teams pulled together, all heaving at the same moment." },
            { 0.55, 0.18, "Layers", "Each layer of blocks is smaller than the one beneath it." },
        }),
        sections = {
            { "Why did the pharaohs want pyramids?", "The pyramids were tombs, built to protect a pharaoh’s body and treasures forever. A pharaoh was a king of ancient Egypt. Egyptians believed that a king lived on after death and needed a safe home for his spirit. Inside, builders made rooms and passages that led to the king’s burial chamber.",
                "The Great Pyramid at Giza was built for King Khufu, about 4,500 years ago. When it was new, it rose about 481 feet, or 146 meters, about as tall as a 40-story building. It is made of more than two million blocks of stone. If you set one block every minute, day and night, you would need about four years to place them all.",
                wow = "Egyptians believed that a king lived on after death and needed a safe home." },
            { "Where did the stone come from?", "Most of the stone for the Great Pyramid came from quarries right next to Giza. A quarry is a place where workers cut stone out of the ground. They used copper chisels, wooden wedges, and hard stone hammers to split the rock into blocks. The finest white limestone, used for the smooth outer coat, came from Tura, across the Nile River.",
                "A crew leader named Merer kept a diary of his boat trips carrying that limestone. His team loaded blocks at Tura and sailed them along canals to a harbor near Giza. His papyrus scrolls are the oldest ones with writing ever found in Egypt. Thanks to Merer, we can follow one stone delivery from start to finish.",
                wow = "Workers split the rock with copper chisels, wooden wedges, and hard stone hammers." },
            { "How do you drag a stone heavier than a car?", "Workers dragged heavy blocks on a sledge, a wooden sled with runners that slide over the ground. Many blocks weighed nearly as much as two cars, and some weighed far more. Teams pulled the sledge with long ropes, all heaving at the same moment. In one modern test, about six men dragged a five-ton block along a wet clay track.",
                "Water was a secret helper. An Egyptian tomb painting shows a worker pouring water on the sand in front of a sledge carrying a giant statue. In 2014, scientists in the Netherlands tested this with a small sledge on a tray of sand. Damp sand cut the pulling force by up to half, because it stays firm and does not heap up in front.",
                wow = "In one modern test, about six men dragged a five-ton block along a wet clay track." },
            { "Why are we still curious about the ramps?", "Builders probably used ramps, because a ramp lets a crew pull a stone up a little at a time. A ramp is a sloping path between a low place and a higher one. The builders set blocks in layers, and each layer was smaller than the one beneath it. A straight ramp could have reached the lower layers easily.",
                "Higher up, a straight ramp would have had to grow very long. Did the builders wrap their ramps around the pyramid, like a path circling a hill? Did they build ramps inside, or lift blocks with levers? Experts still argue, because the builders cleared most of their ramps away. The clues leave room for more than one answer.",
                wow = "Each layer of a pyramid is smaller than the one beneath it." },
        },
